@page "/help"
@* Short answers to what people ask. Keep this in step with the app: a feature that is added or
   changed should have its answer added or changed here in the same pull request. The deploy renders it
   to plain HTML for search engines and llms.txt (tools/static-pages/render.cs), without a browser, so
   calls into JavaScript stay in OnAfterRenderAsync. *@
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject AccountService Account

<PageTitle>Help · Stikling</PageTitle>

<a class="back-link" href="settings">Settings</a>
<h1>Help</h1>

<p class="help-intro">
    Short answers to the questions that come up most. Tap a question to open it.
</p>

<nav class="help-toc" aria-label="Topics">
    @foreach (var (id, title) in topics)
    {
        <a class="chip" href="help#@id">@title</a>
    }
</nav>

<section class="help-section" id="start">
    <h2>Getting started</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is Stikling for?</summary>
        <p>
            Keeping track of your houseplants and the new plants you grow from them, with a history of
            notes and photos for each one. It also keeps a care log, pest treatments, and the pots, soil
            mixes and fertilisers you use, but all of that is optional. A plant with just a name is fine.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is a propagation?</summary>
        <p>
            A new plant you are growing from one you already have, or from seeds. A cutting in a glass of
            water is the usual example. Others are a corm, an offset or pup, a leaf, a division, an air
            layer, and seeds. Stikling calls all of them propagations and keeps them apart from your plants
            until they have rooted and you pot them up.
        </p>
        <p>
            <em>Stikling</em> is the Danish word for a cutting.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Where do I start?</summary>
        <p>
            Add a plant from Plants with the + button. A name is all it needs, and the rest
            can be filled in later. A plant's page opens on its photos and notes, with buttons for <em>Photo</em>,
            <em>Note</em> and <em>Care</em>. When you take a cutting from it, tap the
            three dots next to them, then <em>Propagate</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Have plants already? Before you've added anything, the Today page also offers to add several at once,
            or to start a plant from old photos so its history goes back to when you got it.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I try it before adding my own plants?</summary>
        <p>
            When nothing has been added yet, Today has a <em>Load an example</em> button. It adds an example
            Monstera with a cutting in water and a young plant grown from an earlier cutting, with photos and notes.
            A row on Today removes it again, along with anything added to it. Plants made from it stay.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Where are the settings?</summary>
        <p>
            Behind the gear at the top of the screen, or at the bottom of the menu on a wide screen. That is where you @(Account.IsAvailable ? "sign in, " : "")save a backup, pick light or dark, and add, rename or merge rooms and tags. The pots, soil mixes and products
            you use are under Supplies.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I see how Stikling is made?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes, it is open source. <em>Source code</em> under About in Settings opens the code on GitHub, where you can
            also report a problem or suggest something.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I put it on my phone's home screen?</summary>
        <p>
            On an iPhone, open Stikling in Safari, tap the Share button, and pick
            <em>Add to Home Screen</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            On Android, open it in Chrome, tap the menu with the three dots, and pick
            <em>Install app</em> or <em>Add to home screen</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
            It then opens like any other app, without the browser around it. It is still the same app and
            the same data, so nothing has to be moved.
        </p>
        <p>
            On Android, a long press on the icon gives shortcuts to add a plant, start a propagation or
            open the pest cases.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I use it on a computer?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes, in any browser, and on a tablet too. On a wide screen the tabs move to a menu down the left
            side, with Help and Settings at the bottom of it. A plant or propagation page shows its details
            next to its history, and the buttons that sit at the bottom of the page on a phone are above the
            history instead. In the lists, the round + button is a button next to the title.
        </p>
        <p>
            @(Account.IsAvailable
                ? "Each device keeps its own plants until you sign in on both, which keeps them the same."
                : "Each device keeps its own plants. To move them, save a backup on one and restore it on the other.")
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Does it work without signal?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes. Once it has been opened, everything it needs is kept on the phone, including the plant
            name suggestions. It only goes online to check for a new version@(Account.IsAvailable ? ", and to sync if you're signed in" : "").
            When there is a new version, a banner at the bottom asks you to reload.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Why did it take a while to open the first time?</summary>
        <p>
            The first visit downloads the whole app, about 4&nbsp;MB, so it can work without signal afterwards. On
            slow mobile data that can take half a minute. After that it opens from the phone in a moment.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="data">
    <h2>Your data and backups</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Where is my data kept?</summary>
        @if (Account.IsAvailable)
        {
            <p>
                On this device, in the browser's own storage. If you sign in, it is also kept on the Stikling
                server, photos included, so your other devices get it too. Without an account, nothing you add
                leaves this device unless you save a backup and move it yourself.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                On this device only, in the browser's own storage. There is no account and no server, so nothing
                you add leaves this device unless you save a backup and move it yourself.
            </p>
        }
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What happens if I clear the browser or delete the app?</summary>
        <p>
            Your plants, propagations and photos go with it. Clearing the site data for Stikling, or
            deleting the installed app, removes everything, and without a backup there is no way to get it
            back.
        </p>
        <p>
            Some browsers also clear data by themselves when the phone runs low on space, or when a site
            hasn't been opened for a while. Installing Stikling to the home screen usually stops that. Under
            Backup and storage in Settings you can see whether the browser has agreed to keep your data, and
            ask it to.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I back up?</summary>
        <p>
            Open Settings, then <em>Backup and storage</em>, and tap <em>Save a backup</em>. You get
            a ZIP file with everything in it,
            photos included. Keep it somewhere other than the phone, like cloud storage or your computer.
        </p>
        @if (Account.IsAvailable)
        {
            <p>
                If you're signed in, the backup fetches the full-size photos this device hasn't opened yet, so
                it can take a while the first time.
            </p>
        }
        <p>
            Today reminds you when you haven't taken a backup, or when the last one is 30 days old.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I get my plants into a spreadsheet?</summary>
        <p>
            Under Backup and storage in Settings, tap <em>Plants as CSV</em> or <em>Propagations as CSV</em>. You get a
            file that Excel and Google Sheets can open. It is only a copy to read and sort, not a backup,
            and it can't be restored into Stikling.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I move to a new phone?</summary>
        @if (Account.IsAvailable)
        {
            <p>
                The easiest way is to sign in on the new phone with the same account. Everything that has synced
                comes over by itself, photos included. A bar at the bottom shows while your things are being
                fetched, and photos can take a little longer to appear. Check that the old phone has finished
                syncing first, on the Account page in Settings.
            </p>
            <p>
                Without an account, save a backup on the old phone, get the file over to the new one, open
                Stikling there and tap <em>Restore a backup</em> under Backup and storage in Settings.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                Save a backup on the old phone, get the file over to the new one, open Stikling there and tap
                <em>Restore a backup</em> under Backup and storage in Settings.
            </p>
        }
        <p>
            A restore adds to what is already there instead of replacing it. Anything missing is added,
            anything deleted since the backup comes back, and where the same thing was changed in both
            places, the newer version wins, so a newer one in the backup replaces the one on the device. Restoring the same backup twice does no harm.
        </p>
        <p>
            Before it restores, Stikling saves a backup of what is on the device now to your downloads, named
            <em>stikling-before-restore</em> with the date. If the restore removed something you wanted, restore
            that file to bring it back. It brings back what was deleted, but not edits the restore replaced with
            newer ones.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I use it on two devices?</summary>
        @if (Account.IsAvailable)
        {
            <p>
                Yes. Sign in with the same account on both, and what you add or change on one shows up on the
                other, photos too.
            </p>
        }
        else
        {
            <p>
                Not at the same time. Each device has its own copy and they don't sync. You can move your data
                across with a backup and a restore whenever you switch.
            </p>
        }
    </details>

    @* Signing in is only offered where the app has somewhere to sign in to *@
    @if (Account.IsAvailable)
    {
        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>Do I need an account?</summary>
            <p>
                No. An account is optional, and everything in Stikling works without one. To make one, open
                Settings, then <em>Sign in</em>, and sign in with Google, Facebook or Discord, or with your
                email.
            </p>
            <p>
                With an account, your plants are kept the same on every device you sign in on. If the phone
                already has plants on it when you sign in, they are added to what your account has, so nothing
                is lost.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>When does it sync?</summary>
            <p>
                When you open the app, a couple of seconds after you change something, when the connection
                comes back, and every few minutes while the app is open. The Account page in Settings shows when
                it last synced, and has <em>Sync now</em>. If something goes wrong, a dot shows on the Settings
                gear.
            </p>
            <p>
                Photos go up from the device they were added on. Every device keeps a small copy of each photo,
                so lists look complete even offline, and fetches the full size when you open one. Once
                fetched, it stays on the device. With a lot of photos, the first sync on a new device takes a
                few minutes, and they fill in as they arrive.
            </p>
            <p>
                Without a connection, everything works as usual and your changes wait on the phone until they
                can be sent. If the same thing was changed on two devices, the newer change wins, the same as
                when a backup is restored.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>Is there a limit on photos?</summary>
            <p>
                Each account has 1 GB for photos on the server, which is roughly 2,500 photos. When it is
                nearly full, the Account page in Settings says how much is used. If it fills up, new photos
                stay on the device they were added on, and
                deleting photos you don't need makes room. The photos on your device aren't limited by this.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>What happens when I sign out?</summary>
            <p>
                You choose whether to keep a copy on this device or remove it. What has synced stays with your
                account either way and comes back when you sign in again.
            </p>
            <p>
                Signing out sends your latest changes first, and tells you if any changes or photos haven't
                reached the server yet. Removing the data would delete those, so save a backup first if you
                want to keep them.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>Can I see what the server has about me?</summary>
            <p>
                Yes. On the Account page in Settings, tap <em>Download your data</em>. You get a ZIP with
                everything the Stikling server holds about you: your plants and everything else you added, your
                photos, your settings, and what the sign-in service has, like your email address. The files
                inside are plain text and open in any text editor.
            </p>
            <p>
                When you delete something, the server removes it and only keeps a note that it was deleted, so
                your other devices delete it too.
            </p>
        </details>

        <details class="help-item">
            <summary>How do I delete my account?</summary>
            <p>
                On the Account page in Settings, tap <em>Delete account</em> at the bottom. It erases everything
                your account has on
                the Stikling server, photos included, and deletes your sign-in. It can't be undone.
            </p>
            <p>
                You choose whether this device keeps what it has. If it does, the app carries on without an
                account, like before you signed in. Your other devices stop syncing and keep what is on them.
            </p>
        </details>
    }

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Who can see my plants?</summary>
        <p>
            @(Account.IsAvailable
                ? "Without an account, nobody but you, since nothing leaves the device. With one, only the people in your collection, and for now that is only you."
                : "Nobody but you, since nothing leaves the device.")
            There are no ads and no tracking. The <a href="privacy">privacy page</a> has the details.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="plants">
    <h2>Plants</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I add a lot of plants at once?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes. On the add plant page, or on Today before you've added anything, tap <em>Quick add</em> or
            <em>Add several at once</em>, then type or paste a list of names, one per
            line or separated by commas. Names the app knows, botanical or everyday ones like snake plant,
            are filled in as genus, species and cultivar, and anything written after them goes into the
            notes. A name it doesn't know becomes the nickname, and so does an everyday name that two plants
            share, like money tree, so you can pick which one it is. Check the list, remove what you don't
            want, and tap the button to add them all.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What are genus, species and cultivar?</summary>
        <p>
            The parts of a plant's botanical name. In <em>Monstera deliciosa</em> 'Thai Constellation',
            Monstera is the genus, deliciosa the species, and Thai Constellation the cultivar, a named
            variety. If you don't know them, type what you call the plant under <em>What plant is it?</em>,
            like snake plant, and pick it from the list to fill them in. Suggestions come up as you type, but
            anything can be typed, and all three can be left empty if you give the plant a nickname.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the name under my plant's botanical name?</summary>
        <p>
            What it's called in everyday words, like Snake plant under <em>Dracaena trifasciata</em>. It
            shows on plants without a nickname. The names come with the app, from Wikidata and from lists
            kept by hand, and a plant the app has no everyday name for only shows its botanical one. Under
            Everyday names in Settings you can switch between English and Danish names.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do rooms and spots work?</summary>
        <p>
            A room is where a plant lives, and a spot is a place inside it, like "Living room / On top of
            the PC". Pick one you have used before so the same room doesn't end up with two names.
            Filtering the plant list by a room includes its spots. Under Rooms and tags in Settings you can add a
            room or spot before any plant is in it, rename or merge them, and remove one that is empty.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What happens when a plant dies or I give it away?</summary>
        <p>
            Open the plant, tap the three dots next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em> and pick <em>It died</em> or
            <em>Gave it away or sold it</em>. For several at once, use <em>Select</em> on the plant list. It leaves the list but keeps its history, and you can still find it under
            <em>Gone</em>. When you mark a plant as died, you can add a note on what happened. It shows
            on the plant and in the <em>Gone</em> list, so you can look it over before buying the same plant again. Under <em>Gone</em>, pick <em>Died</em> to see only the plants that died and why.
            When you add a plant with the same name as one that died, the form reminds you of it and what went wrong.
            For a plant you gave away or sold, you can note who got it and what it was sold or swapped for,
            for example a price or the plant you got back. An offspring that was given away or sold shows who got it in the family list.
            If it comes back, or you picked it by mistake, the same place has <em>Back in the collection</em>.
            Deleting a plant removes it along with its history, so saying what happened is usually the better choice.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>I have two of the same plant. Do I type it all in twice?</summary>
        <p>
            No. Open the first one, tap <em>Details</em> and use <em>Add another like this</em>. The add
            form opens with the same names, room, origin, source, price paid, medium, soil mix and tags. The nickname, pots,
            notes and photos are left empty, since those belong to each plant on its own, and the date starts at today. If you got them together, a button under the date switches it to the first plant's date.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you start a new plant from scratch with a name you already have, the form says so and links
            to the one you have, in case you meant to open that instead.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What do dormant and quarantine mean?</summary>
        <p>
            Dormant is for a plant that is resting, like an alocasia dropping its leaves for the winter, so it
            doesn't look dead. Quarantine is for a new or sick plant you are keeping apart from the rest.
            Both are started and ended with the three dots next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em> on the plant's page. They show
            as a badge, have a filter on the plant list, and are written on the plant's history when they
            start and end. A new plant can go straight into quarantine under <em>More details</em> when you
            add it. A quarantine lasts 14 days unless you pick another length. When it is up, the plant
            shows on Today so you can check it and let it out.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I log watering and feeding?</summary>
        <p>
            Open the plant and tap <em>Care</em>, next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em>. It shows when it was last watered, fed and
            so on, and logging is a tap. Under that, <em>The last year</em> is a calendar with a row for each month, showing the days it was
            watered, fed or had a meter reading, so gaps in the rhythm stand out. Tap a month to list the days in it. When it was last watered also shows under the plant's name. To log for several plants at once, tap <em>Select</em> on the plant list, pick
            them and tap <em>Log care</em>. The same bar can also add a note or tags to all of them, and
            <em>Move</em> puts them all in a room or spot. Plants already there are left alone. If you have
            a meter, tap <em>Add EC and pH</em> when logging a feed to note the readings, and they show on
            that line in the care history.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How does the watering reminder work?</summary>
        <p>
            Turn it on under <em>Watering reminder</em> in Settings. It learns how often each plant gets water from what
            you have logged, and asks you to check the plant on Today when it is probably dry. It never tells you to water,
            and it never uses a care guide or a fixed number of days. It says nothing about a plant until it has seen a few
            waterings, so the first weeks are quiet. Until then the plant's page says how many more it needs. Log
            waterings as you do them and it gets better. It leans later when unsure, since a plant that is checked a day too
            late is better off than one watered too soon.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you log meter readings, it learns how long the plant takes to dry down to the level you water at, and that
            works as soon as one watering has been followed by a dry reading. This is for plants you water from the top.
            Semi-hydro (LECA and PON), corm risers, self-watering pots and plants with an outer pot you keep water in
            count how long the reservoir lasts instead, and the row says <em>Refilled</em> when you tick it.
            A plant in water is topped up, and the row says <em>Topped up</em>. When the way a plant gets water changes,
            like moving from soil to LECA, it starts learning again, since the old waterings say nothing about the new way.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What do Still wet and No meter do on Today?</summary>
        <p>
            For a plant you water from the top, tap <em>Still wet</em> and pick what the meter reads, from 1 to 10. The reading
            is saved with the plant's care, and the plant comes back later, once it has had time to dry. <em>No meter</em> puts it
            off for a few days without a reading. For the other plants the button says <em>Not yet</em> and does the same. The tick logs the
            watering, refill or top up for today. The clock puts the row off for a day or more, like the other rows.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the Water at reading?</summary>
        <p>
            It is the meter reading you water at, from 1 to 10, under <em>Watering</em> when you edit a plant that is watered
            from the top. It is 3 unless you change it, which is the top of the red dry part on most meters. Set it higher
            for a plant you keep moister, like a peace lily. To leave one plant out of the reminder, switch off <em>Remind me to water</em>
            there. It stays out of Today until you switch it back on.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Does the reminder know about the seasons and grow lights?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes. Plants drink less as the days get shorter, so when the days are shortening the reminder leans later for a plant
            that has no light on it, and it uses the same weeks of earlier years when you have them. A plant
            under a grow light doesn't slow down in winter, so mark the room or spot as having one under Rooms and tags in Settings.
            Plants there are left out of the seasons. It also keeps the stretches a plant is dormant apart from the rest, so a
            plant's slow winter doesn't make the reminder late in spring.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What are tags for?</summary>
        <p>
            Your own labels, like "variegated", "rare" or "for swap". Picking tags on the plant list shows
            the plants that have all of them, and tapping a tag on a plant's page does the same. You can make a tag under Rooms and tags in Settings by
            picking the plants it goes on. There a tag can also be renamed, or merged into another one.
            Adding or removing a tag is written on the plant's history, so a tag like "flowering" still shows when it
            started and when you took it off.
        </p>
    </details>
    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I keep a plant at the top of the list?</summary>
        <p>
            Open the plant and tap the star next to its name. Starred plants stay at the top of the list
            whichever way you sort it, and the sort you picked still applies within the starred plants and
            within the rest. Tap the star again to take it off.
        </p>
    </details>
    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I sort the plant list?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes, with the dropdown next to the filters. A to Z is the default. Newest puts the plants you
            added last at the top, By room groups them by where they stand, and Last activity puts the
            plants you last watered, fed or wrote something on first. The list keeps the sort you picked
            the next time you open it on this device.
        </p>
    </details>
    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the light setting for?</summary>
        <p>
            It notes how much light the plant gets where it stands: low, medium, bright indirect or some
            direct sun. It is optional, and you can set it under <em>More details</em> when you add or edit
            the plant. Adding another plant
            like one you already have copies it.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="propagations">
    <h2>Propagations</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I start one?</summary>
        <p>
            Open the plant you took it from and tap <em>Propagate</em>, so the new plant stays linked to it.
            For something without a parent plant, like bought seeds, use the + button on
            Propagate.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What do the stages mean?</summary>
        <p>
            <strong>Started</strong> is the day you put it in water or a medium.
            <strong>Rooting</strong> is when the first roots show.
            <strong>Rooted</strong> is when it has roots enough to be potted up.
            To move it on, tap the stage button next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em> on the propagation's page and pick the next
            one. Moving it to Rooting there also notes today as the day of the first root, and the dates
            can be changed with <em>Edit</em>. For seeds the same stages are called Sown, Germinating and
            Sprouted.
        </p>
        <p>
            A propagation is finished once all of it has been potted up or has failed. It is then
            <strong>Done</strong> if any of it made it, and <strong>Failed</strong> if none did.
        </p>
        <p>
            A finished propagation shows how it turned out: how many days it ran, the day of the first
            root, how many were potted up, and the plants it became with the room each one is in now.
        </p>
        <p>
            Marked one as failed or potted one up by mistake? Edit the propagation and correct the numbers
            under <em>Progress</em>. Any left over go back to growing, and the history keeps a note of the
            correction.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What if I have several cuttings in one glass?</summary>
        <p>
            Give the propagation a count, like 3 corms in LECA. They are tracked as one batch, and you can
            pot up or mark failed one or a few at a time as each goes its own way.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I track seeds?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes. Pick Seed as the type and put the number of seeds you sowed in <em>How many</em>. The
            sowing date is the day you started it. Moving it to Germinating notes the day the first one came
            up. To say how many have come up, edit the propagation and fill it in under <em>Progress</em>. It then shows how many came up, as a percentage.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What does Pot up do?</summary>
        <p>
            It turns one or more from the propagation into plants in your collection. You find it, and
            <em>Mark failed</em>, behind the stage button on the propagation's page. Each new plant keeps
            a link to the plant the propagation came from, so you can always see where it started.
            Both ask for a date, today unless you change it, so something you only found out a week later
            is recorded on the right day.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How does the family tree work?</summary>
        <p>
            Under a plant's name you see what it came from and how many propagations and plants have come
            from it. Tap one of them, or <em>Details</em>, for the whole family. It follows the links across
            generations, so a cutting of a cutting still leads back to the first plant. The family list on a plant's page also
            adds up what it has given: how many cuttings were taken and how many rooted, how many plants were given
            away or sold, and how many plants have come from those in turn.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What do LECA, PON, sphagnum, corm riser and air layer mean?</summary>
        <p><strong>LECA:</strong> @Labels.Explain(GrowingMedium.Leca)</p>
        <p><strong>PON:</strong> @Labels.Explain(GrowingMedium.Pon)</p>
        <p><strong>Sphagnum:</strong> @Labels.Explain(GrowingMedium.Sphagnum)</p>
        <p><strong>Corm riser:</strong> @Labels.Explain(GrowingMedium.CormRiser)</p>
        <p><strong>Air layer:</strong> @Labels.Explain(PropagationType.AirLayer)</p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I compare mediums, like water and perlite?</summary>
        <p>
            Give the batches you started together the same name under Experiment, in <em>More details</em>
            when you start or edit them. The
            Propagations list then shows them side by side, with a bar for how long each took to root and
            the dates of the first root and first leaf. For corms you can note the size when editing, and it
            shows on each row, so bigger and smaller corms can be compared.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is on the results page?</summary>
        <p>
            <em>Success rate and days to root</em> on the Propagations list shows how each medium, each type
            and each rooting aid has done across everything you have propagated. Once three batches of the
            same type in the same medium have rooted, a propagation still going also says whether it is on
            time or slower than usual.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="today">
    <h2>Today</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What shows up on Today?</summary>
        <p>
            Pest treatments that are due, anything you have flagged, plants whose quarantine is up, propagations nobody has looked at for
            a week, plants to check for water and the monthly photo reminder if they are turned on, an old photo of a plant from this day in an earlier year, and under Lately, what you wrote down in the last 30 days.
            It also reminds you to back up.
        </p>
        <p>
            The line at the top counts your plants and the propagations going. Once something has been potted up or has
            failed this month, it also says how many of those units were potted up. On the installed app, the icon shows
            the number of things due on Today. It is updated each time you open Today.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I get a propagation off "Worth a look"?</summary>
        <p>
            Add a note or a photo to it, or move its stage. Anything on its history counts as looking at it.
            If there is nothing to say yet, tap the clock on the row to put it off until tomorrow, for 3 days
            or for a week. It comes back by itself after that.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I flag something to do later?</summary>
        <p>
            Tap the three dots next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em> on a plant or propagation, then <em>Flag it</em>, and say
            what it needs, like "Repot soon". It stays on Today under Needs attention until you tick it off
            there or on its page.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the photo reminder?</summary>
        <p>
            Once a month, Today lists the plants and propagations that haven't had a photo that month, room
            by room, with a camera button on each. It keeps every history growing. It is off until you turn
            it on in Settings.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Why is there an old photo on Today?</summary>
        <p>
            When a plant has a photo taken on this day a year ago (or two or three), or six months ago,
            Today shows it next to the plant's newest photo, so you can see how much it has grown. The
            newest photo has to be at least 30 days newer, and only one plant is shown, the one with the
            longest gap. Tap <em>Before and after</em> to make a card of the two to share. The card says how long ago it was, like <em>A year ago today</em>. It goes away
            on its own tomorrow.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="history">
    <h2>Photos and history</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is on a plant's page?</summary>
        <p>
            Its history, which is what the page opens on. Everything that happened to a plant or
            propagation, newest first: your notes, your photos, and
            changes the app wrote down by itself, like a new stage or a move to another room. Tap a photo to
            see it full screen, choose which one is the cover and frame it. Where it came from, where it stands and
            what it grows in are under <em>Details</em>, and <em>Edit</em> changes them.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I change the photo at the top, or get the plant in frame?</summary>
        <p>
            Tap the photo at the top of the page, or any photo in the history, to see it full screen. There,
            <em>Set as cover</em> makes it the one at the top and on the cards. <em>Frame</em> lets you drag
            the photo and zoom in so the plant sits well where the photo is cropped, on cards, at the top of
            the page and in the history. The photo itself isn't changed, and full screen always shows all of it.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I add old photos from before I used the app?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes. Tap <em>Photo</em> on its page and choose them from your photos. Each photo lands on the day it was
            taken, read from the photo itself, so the history starts from the beginning. If some photos
            have no date of their own, the app says how many so you can check them.
        </p>
        <p>
            For a plant you haven't added yet, pick them with <em>Gallery</em> when adding it, or tap
            <em>start from old photos</em> on Today before you've added anything. <em>When you got it</em> then
            moves back to the day of the oldest photo, unless you've already changed it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Starting a propagation works the same way: add its photos with <em>Camera</em> or <em>Gallery</em> on the form.
            The newest becomes the cover, and <em>Started on</em> moves back to the day of the oldest photo.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I share a plant or a cutting?</summary>
        <p>
            Tap <em>Share</em> on its page. Stikling makes one picture with a photo, its name, a plain line about it
            like "Cutting from my Monstera, rooted in 18 days in LECA", and stikling.app small in a corner. Choose
            square, 4:5 or 9:16 to fit where you are posting, and pick which of its photos to use. On a phone,
            <em>Share</em> opens the share menu, so the picture goes straight to Instagram, Messages or wherever you
            choose. Where the browser can't do that, <em>Save image</em> keeps it as a picture you can post yourself.
        </p>
        <p>
            The line is written from what the app has kept, and it only says what it knows. You can change the name
            and the line before sharing. Nothing you change is saved, and nothing leaves your device until you
            share the picture. With no photo, or by choosing <em>No photo</em>, you get a card built around the
            biggest number, like how many days it took to root.
        </p>
        <p>
            With two or more photos you can also make a before and after. Tap <em>Share</em>, then
            <em>Before and after</em>. It puts two photos side by side with the date on each and how long
            apart they were. It starts on the first and the newest photo, and you can change either one.
        </p>
        <p>
            With three or more photos you can make a time-lapse video. Tap <em>Share</em>, then <em>Time-lapse</em>.
            Each photo shows for a moment with its date and day count, in the order they were taken, and fades into
            the next. Choose <em>Slow</em>, <em>Normal</em> or <em>Fast</em>, and the size, and the preview plays
            on a loop. Tap a photo in the row to leave it out, and tap it again to put it back. At least two stay in, and a video takes up to 60
            photos, so a longer history starts with 60 spread over it.
            Then tap <em>Make video</em>. It is made on your phone, without sound, so it takes a little while, and
            you can cancel it. When it is done, <em>Share video</em> opens the share menu and <em>Save video</em>
            keeps it as an MP4 you can post on TikTok or Reels. If you change anything after that, you make it again.
            Firefox on Android makes videos more slowly than other browsers.
        </p>
        <p>
            Photos show which day they were taken on, like <em>3 May 2026 · day 24</em>. A propagation counts
            from the day it started, and a plant from the day you got it, so that needs a full date for when
            you got it. Photos taken before that day get no count.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Can I share a link to a plant's page?</summary>
        <p>
            Yes, if you're signed in. Tap <em>Share</em> on its page, then <em>Link</em>, then <em>Make link</em>.
            You get a link to a page anyone can open, without the app or an account, with its photos, milestones
            and notes from the first day to now. Post it with a cutting's progress, or send it to the friend who
            gave it to you. The page follows the plant, so new photos and notes show up on it after the next sync.
        </p>
        <p>
            You choose what is on it. Switch off <em>Show my notes</em> to keep your notes to yourself, tap a photo
            to leave it out, and change the name and the line under it. The line starts as the one on the share
            card, like "Cutting from my Monstera", so change it if that says more than you want. The page never
            shows the room, the price, the shop or person it came from, who it went to, its tags, pots or care.
            While notes are shown, the note box on that plant says so.
        </p>
        <p>
            <em>Turn off the link</em> stops the page within a few minutes, and so does deleting the plant, and anyone who opens the link sees that it is
            gone. Turning it on again makes a new link. A link needs an account because the page is shown from the
            Stikling server, using the photos your device has uploaded.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I fix a note with the wrong date?</summary>
        <p>
            Tap the pencil next to a note or photo in its history to change its text or date. The earlier version is kept and shown
            under <em>edited</em>. To write something down that happened earlier, tap
            <em>Happened earlier?</em> before adding the note or photo.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="pests">
    <h2>Pests</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is a pest case?</summary>
        <p>
            One outbreak, like spider mites in the living room. A case covers the whole collection, one room,
            or the plants you pick, because pests spread and are treated together. You say how often to
            treat, and Today shows when the next round is due.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Where is Pests when I have no case open?</summary>
        <p>
            Pests only gets a tab while a case is being treated or watched. The rest of the
            time it is under <em>Supplies</em>, with your old cases. To open a case for one plant, tap the
            three dots next to <em>Photo</em> and <em>Note</em> on its page, then <em>Found pests</em>.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the difference between treating and watching?</summary>
        <p>
            While a case is <strong>Treating</strong>, each treatment you log moves the next one along by the
            interval, and the case shows when the next one is due once the first is logged. Until then, Today
            and the Pests list show the case as having no treatment yet. Set it to <strong>Watching</strong> when the pests seem gone, and Today asks for a check
            once a week instead. If a check finds some, <em>Found some</em> puts it back to treating.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What are sticky trap counts?</summary>
        <p>
            Count what is on a yellow or blue sticky trap now and then, and the app works out how many were
            caught since last time. The chart of the catch per week shows whether the treatment is working.
            Thrips, fungus gnats and whitefly fly into traps, so their cases show the trap straight away.
            Other pests rarely end up on one, but if you have a trap up anyway, tap <em>Count a sticky trap</em>
            on the case.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

<section class="help-section" id="supplies">
    <h2>Pots, mixes and fertilisers</h2>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>Do I have to fill in pots, soil mixes and products?</summary>
        <p>
            No. They are all under Supplies for anyone who wants them. Once they are there, a plant can pick
            its pot and mix from a list, and logging a feed says what went in the water without typing. A
            propagation that grows in soil can pick a mix too, and it goes on to the plants when you pot up.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What can I do with a soil mix?</summary>
        <p>
            Save it under a name with what is in it. On the Soil mixes page, tap the number of plants next
            to a mix to see which plants are in it, and tap a plant to open it. If every ingredient has an
            amount, tap Measure out, type how much mix you want, and the app says how much of each
            ingredient to use.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What is the difference between a product and a feed?</summary>
        <p>
            A product is one bottle or bag, like a fertiliser or silica, with the dose you usually use. A
            feed is several products mixed into the water together, in the order they go in. Fill in how
            much water you are mixing, and the app says how much of each to add. Tap the line saying how
            many plants a feed has been given to, and it lists the plants and the days they got it.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What are nursery pots and outer pots?</summary>
        <p>
            A nursery pot is the plain one the plant actually sits in, and an outer pot is the decorative one
            without drainage that it goes inside. Some pots are neither and stand on their own. The Pots page
            lists one kind at a time, and shows which of yours are in use and which are free. Picking a
            self-watering pot for a plant sets "Grows in" to LECA, and you can change it after.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>How do I tell similar pots apart?</summary>
        <p>
            Give each one a photo. Tap <em>Photo</em> beside a pot on the Pots page, or add photos while
            adding or editing it. The photo then shows beside the pot there, on the chips when you pick a pot
            for a plant, and on the page of each plant that uses it, where tapping it shows the pot's photos.
            The photos belong to the pot, not to a plant, so they stay with it when it moves to another plant.
            Open a pot's photos on the Pots page to choose which one is shown, frame it or delete one.
            Deleting a pot deletes its photos too.
        </p>
    </details>

    <details class="help-item">
        <summary>What are treatment recipes?</summary>
        <p>
            Sprays and drenches you make up yourself against pests, like an alcohol spray. Save one under
            Supplies, and logging a treatment is then a tap. <em>Mix a batch</em> works out the amounts for
            any size of bottle.
        </p>
    </details>
</section>

@code {
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender)
            return;

        await using var module = await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/page.js");
        await module.InvokeVoidAsync("scrollToFragment");
    }

    private static readonly (string Id, string Title)[] topics =
    [
        ("start", "Getting started"),
        ("data", "Data and backups"),
        ("plants", "Plants"),
        ("propagations", "Propagations"),
        ("today", "Today"),
        ("history", "History"),
        ("pests", "Pests"),
        ("supplies", "Supplies")
    ];
}
